.Sh NAME
.Nm wattle
.Nd the Wattle programming language
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Wattle is a dynamic language with Clojure-like syntax.
A program is parsed and compiled into bytecode that is run on
the Wattle virtual machine.
.Pp
Wattle’s virtual machine and core library derive from Janet.
The parser accepts Wattle source,
not Janet source.
Source files have the extension
.Ql "\&.wattle" .
This page describes the language;
.Xr wattle 1
describes the command-line program.
.Pp
A small program illustrates definitions,
functions,
collections and calls:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdefn greeting \(lBname\(rB
  \*(lpstring \(dqHello, \(dq name \(dq!\(dq\*(rp\*(rp

\*(lpdefn main \(lBscript & args\(rB
  \*(lpeach name \*(lpif \*(lpempty? args\*(rp \(lB\(dqworld\(dq\(rB args\*(rp
    \*(lpprint \*(lpgreeting name\*(rp\*(rp\*(rp\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
Running this file with
.Xr wattle 1
from the command line prints
.Ql "Hello, <argument>!"
for each command line argument,
or
.Ql "Hello, world!"
if there are no arguments.
.
.Sh SOURCE FORMS
Wattle source consists of forms,
whitespace and comments.
The forms below include tokens,
byte sequences,
collections and tuples.
Prefixes modify the form that follows them.
.
.Ss Comments
A semicolon begins a comment that ends at the end of the line.
A
.Ql "#!"
at the first byte of a file also begins a shebang comment,
for a script’s interpreter line.
.
.Ss Whitespace
Whitespace includes spaces,
tabs,
line breaks and commas.
Commas may separate forms,
as in
.Ql "{:a 1, :b 2}" ;
they are never part of a symbol.
.
.Ss Tokens
Tokens comprise literal tokens,
numbers,
symbols and keywords.
.Pp
There are three literal tokens:
.Ql "true" ,
.Ql "false"
and
.Ql "nil" .
In a conditional test,
only
.Ql "false"
and
.Ql "nil"
are false.
Zero,
an empty string and empty collections are all treated as true.
.Pp
Numbers are double-precision floating-point values.
Decimal,
exponent,
hexadecimal and radix forms include
.Ql "42" ,
.Ql "-1\&.5" ,
.Ql "1e3" ,
.Ql "0x1F"
and
.Ql "2r101" .
Underscores may separate digits,
as in
.Ql "1_000" .
There is no separate integer literal type.
.Ql "int/s64"
and
.Ql "int/u64"
construct exact 64-bit integer values when needed.
.Pp
A symbol is a name formed from ASCII letters,
digits,
valid non-ASCII UTF-8 and the characters
.Ql "!$%&*+-\&./:<=>?@^_" .
Examples include
.Ql "total" ,
.Ql "string/split"
and
.Ql "empty?" .
A symbol cannot begin with a digit or colon.
At the start of a form,
.Ql "@"
and
.Ql "^"
are parse errors;
reserve
.Ql "\&!"
for mutable literals.
A token that reads as a number is a number.
Symbols are resolved as bindings unless quoted.
.Pp
A keyword begins with a colon,
as in
.Ql ":name" .
Keywords are values,
commonly used as map keys.
.
.Ss Byte sequences
A string is a sequence of bytes delimited by double quotes.
Strings are immutable.
A string can span lines,
and a line break in it is part of the string.
Strings can include escapes like
.Ql "\en" ,
.Ql "\et" ,
.Ql "\e\(dq"
and
.Ql "\e\e" ;
.Ql "\exHH"
inserts a byte,
and
.Ql "\euXXXX"
inserts a Unicode code point encoded as UTF-8.
.Pp
A buffer uses
.Ql "\&!"
immediately before its opening double quotes.
Buffers are mutable byte sequences and accept the same escapes
as strings.
.Pp
Three or more double quotes delimit a
.Em raw
string or buffer.
Raw byte sequences may span lines and do not process escapes.
A line break immediately after the opening quotes and one before
the closing quotes are removed.
If every content line has the opening quotes’ indentation,
that indentation is removed from each line.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lptype \(dqa\enb\(dq\*(rp                ; :string
\*(lptype \(dq\(dq\(dqraw\en\(dq\(dq\(dq\*(rp           ; :string
\*(lptype !\(dqa\enb\(dq\*(rp               ; :buffer
\*(lptype !\(dq\(dq\(dqraw\en\(dq\(dq\(dq\*(rp          ; :buffer

\*(lplength \(dqe\(dq\*(rp                 ; 1 byte
\*(lplength \(dqé\(dq\*(rp                 ; 2 bytes

\*(lpdef text
  \(dq\(dq\(dq
  first
  second
  \(dq\(dq\(dq\*(rp
\*(lp= text \(dqfirst\ensecond\(dq\*(rp     ; true
\*(lp= \(dq\e\en\(dq \(dq\(dq\(dq\en\(dq\(dq\(dq\*(rp           ; true: raw \en is two bytes
.Ed
.
.Ss Collections
Wattle includes five built-in collections:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\(lBa b\(rB         ; immutable vector
{:a :b}       ; immutable map
#{:a :b}      ; immutable set

!\(lBa b\(rB        ; mutable array
!{:a :b}      ; mutable table
.Ed
.Pp
Vectors,
arrays and sets store elements.
Maps and tables store key-value pairs.
Each element,
key and value is an expression evaluated when the collection
is constructed.
Quoting a form gives its syntax as data.
.
.Ss Function calls
Parentheses delimit a tuple.
A tuple is an immutable sequence of values.
In executable source,
the compiler treats a tuple as a function call,
a special form or a macro invocation.
In
.Ql "\*(lp+ 1 2\*(rp" ,
.Ql "+"
is called with two arguments and returns 3.
The function position may itself contain a form:
.Ql "\*(lp\*(lpfn \(lBx\(rB \*(lp* x x\*(rp\*(rp 4\*(rp"
returns 16.
.Pp
Tuples are primarily used to represent Wattle code rather than
general-purpose sequential data;
use a vector for ordinary sequential data.
Quoting a tuple produces its value without compiling it as a
call:
.Ql "\(aq\*(lp+ 1 2\*(rp"
contains the symbol
.Ql "+"
and the numbers 1 and 2.
.
.Ss Prefixes
Four prefixes modify the form that follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\(aqx     ; shorthand for \*(lpquote x\*(rp
`x     ; shorthand for \*(lpquasiquote x\*(rp
~x     ; shorthand for \*(lpunquote x\*(rp
|x     ; shorthand for \*(lpsplice x\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
The form after a prefix must start on the same line.
.
.Sh COLLECTIONS
Wattle includes immutable and mutable collections.
.
.Ss Mutability
Vectors,
maps and sets are persistent,
immutable collections.
Operations that add,
remove or replace values return an updated collection and leave
the original available.
The result may share unchanged structure with the original,
so an update need not copy the entire collection.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdef xs \(lB1 2\(rB\*(rp
\*(lpdef ys \*(lpconj xs 3\*(rp\*(rp
xs                    ; \(lB1 2\(rB
ys                    ; \(lB1 2 3\(rB
\*(lpassoc {:a 1} :b 2\*(rp   ; {:a 1 :b 2}
.Ed
.Pp
Arrays and tables are mutable.
Operations such as
.Ql "put!"
and
.Ql "array/push!"
change them in place:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdef items !\(lB1 2\(rB\*(rp
\*(lparray/push! items 3\*(rp
items                 ; !\(lB1 2 3\(rB
.Ed
.Pp
Arrays and tables compare by identity.
Vectors,
maps and sets compare by their contents.
.
.Ss Accessing
Vectors and arrays are indexed from zero.
.Ql "get"
reads a position or key and accepts an optional default:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpget \(lB10 20\(rB 1\*(rp        ; 20
\*(lpget \(lB10 20\(rB 2 :oops\*(rp  ; :oops
.Ed
.Pp
Map and table literals alternate key and value expressions.
Neither type can store
.Ql "nil"
or NaN as a key.
Storing
.Ql "nil"
as a value removes the entry.
Set elements also cannot be
.Ql "nil"
or NaN,
and duplicate elements count once.
Map and set iteration order is unspecified;
do not rely on the order of expression evaluation inside their
literals.
.Ql "has-value?"
checks set membership:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpget {:name \(dqAda\(dq} :name\*(rp        ; \(dqAda\(dq
\*(lpget {:name \(dqAda\(dq} :age :oops\*(rp   ; :oops
\*(lphas-value? #{1 2} 2\*(rp            ; true
.Ed
.Pp
A collection can also be called with an index or key to look
up a value:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lp\(lB10 20\(rB 1\*(rp             ; 20
\*(lp{:name \(dqAda\(dq} :name\*(rp   ; \(dqAda\(dq
\*(lp#{1 2 3} 2\*(rp            ; 2
.Ed
.
.Sh BINDINGS AND CONTROL FLOW
.Ql "def"
binds a value to a name that cannot be reassigned.
.Ql "var"
binds a value to a name that
.Ql "set"
can reassign.
Both accept destructuring patterns.
.Ql "do"
evaluates its forms in order and returns the last result.
The core library offers the
.Ql "let"
macro that uses these to create bindings within a specified
scope:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdef raise 7\*(rp
\*(lpvar total raise\*(rp
\*(lpset total \*(lp+ total 3\*(rp\*(rp
\*(lplet \(lBx 4, y 5\(rB
  \*(lp+ x y total\*(rp\*(rp          ; 19
.Ed
.
.Ss Patterns
.Ql "def" ,
.Ql "var" ,
.Ql "let"
and function parameters can destructure values.
Vector and tuple patterns bind by position.
A map pattern writes the binding first and the key second:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdef \(lBfirst second & rest\(rB \(lB1 2 3 4\(rB\*(rp
\*(lpdef {name :name} {:name \(dqAda\(dq}\*(rp
\*(lpdef {:keys \(lBx y\(rB} {:x 1 :y 2}\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
In a map pattern,
.Ql ":keys \(lBx y\(rB"
selects keyword keys.
.Ql ":strs"
and
.Ql ":syms"
select string and symbol keys;
.Ql ":as"
binds the whole value;
and
.Ql ":or {x default}"
supplies a default when
.Ql "x"
is
.Ql "nil" .
Defaults are evaluated only when used.
.
.Ss Conditionals
.Ql "if"
evaluates its test and one branch:
.Ql "\*(lpif test then else\*(rp" .
The core library defines
.Ql "when"
for a conditional body,
.Ql "cond"
for successive tests and
.Ql "case"
for comparing one value with several candidates:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpif \*(lp> total 0\*(rp :positive :nonpositive\*(rp  ; :positive
\*(lpwhen \*(lp> total 0\*(rp :positive\*(rp             ; :positive
\*(lpcond \*(lp< total 0\*(rp :negative
      \*(lp= total 0\*(rp :zero
      :positive\*(rp                         ; :positive
\*(lpcase total 0 :zero 10 :ten :other\*(rp      ; :ten
.Ed
.
.Ss Loops
.Ql "while"
tests its condition before each repetition and evaluates its
body while the condition is truthy.
.Ql "break"
exits the loop.
The core library defines
.Ql "loop" ,
.Ql "for"
and
.Ql "each"
for iteration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpeach item \(lB1 2 3\(rB
  \*(lpprint item\*(rp\*(rp
.Ed
.
.Sh FUNCTIONS
.Ql "fn"
creates a function.
The core library defines
.Ql "defn"
to bind a named function with optional metadata and a docstring.
Parameter lists are vectors.
A symbol after
.Ql "&"
receives the remaining arguments as a vector.
Functions are values:
they may be passed,
stored and returned,
and they can refer to bindings in their enclosing scope.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdef double \*(lpfn \(lBx\(rB \*(lp* x 2\*(rp\*(rp\*(rp
\*(lpdouble 3\*(rp                ; 6

\*(lpdefn sum \(lBxs\(rB
  \*(lpreduce + 0 xs\*(rp\*(rp

\*(lpdefn add-all \(lB& numbers\(rB
  \*(lpsum numbers\*(rp\*(rp

\*(lpadd-all 1 2 3\*(rp           ; 6
.Ed
.Pp
A function can have several arities.
Each clause begins with a parameter vector.
A call selects the clause for its argument count.
Clauses cannot duplicate or skip counts,
and at most one may use
.Ql "&" :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdefn greet
  \(dqReturns a greeting\&.\(dq
  \*(lp\(lB\(rB \*(lpgreet \(dqworld\(dq\*(rp\*(rp
  \*(lp\(lBname\(rB \*(lpstring \(dqHello, \(dq name \(dq!\(dq\*(rp\*(rp\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
For named options,
a map pattern after
.Ql "&"
receives keyword and value pairs.
A final map may supply or override those pairs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdefn greet-with \(lBname & {:keys \(lBprefix\(rB :or {prefix \(dqHello\(dq}}\(rB
  \*(lpstring prefix \(dq, \(dq name \(dq!\(dq\*(rp\*(rp

\*(lpgreet-with \(dqAda\(dq :prefix \(dqHi\(dq\*(rp    ; \(dqHi, Ada!\(dq
\*(lpgreet-with \(dqAda\(dq {:prefix \(dqHi\(dq}\*(rp  ; \(dqHi, Ada!\(dq
.Ed
.Pp
A short function has the form
.Ql "#\*(lp\&.\&.\&.\*(rp" .
Within it,
.Ql "$"
or
.Ql "$0"
is the first argument,
.Ql "$1"
the second,
and
.Ql "$&"
the remaining arguments after the highest numbered argument
used:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpmap #\*(lp* $ 2\*(rp \(lB1 2 3\(rB\*(rp  ; !\(lB2 4 6\(rB
.Ed
.
.Sh MACROS
A macro receives its arguments as unevaluated forms and returns
a form for the compiler.
The core library provides
.Ql "defmacro"
for defining macros.
Quasiquote (\c
.Ql "`" )
constructs a form.
Within it,
.Ql "~x"
inserts one form,
while
.Ql "~|args"
inserts each form from a sequence:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpdefmacro twice \(lBx\(rB
  `\*(lpdo ~x ~x\*(rp\*(rp

\*(lpdefmacro call-with \(lBf & args\(rB
  `\*(lp~f ~|args\*(rp\*(rp

\*(lpvar calls 0\*(rp
\*(lptwice \*(lpset calls \*(lp+ calls 1\*(rp\*(rp\*(rp
calls                            ; 2
\*(lpcall-with + 1 2\*(rp                ; 3
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql "twice"
inserts its argument twice,
so the argument is evaluated twice when the expanded form runs.
.Pp
Parentheses in generated code are tuples.
Vectors,
maps and sets remain collections in the syntax tree,
so a macro can inspect the form written in source.
.Ql "tuple"
can be used to construct a call without quasiquote.
.
.Sh MODULES
A module can be a Wattle source file,
an image or a native library.
.Ql "import"
loads a module and adds its public bindings to the current environment
with a prefix derived from the module name.
.Ql "use"
adds the bindings without a prefix.
.Pp
If
.Ql "greetings\&.wattle"
defines
.Ql "hello" ,
either of these forms makes it available:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpimport \&./greetings\*(rp
\*(lpgreetings/hello \(dqAda\(dq\*(rp

\*(lpuse \&./greetings\*(rp
\*(lphello \(dqAda\(dq\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql "import"
also accepts
.Ql ":as"
or
.Ql ":prefix"
to choose the prefix.
.Ev WATTLE_PREFIX
selects the directory for system modules;
see
.Xr wattle 1 .
.
.Sh ERRORS
.Ql "\*(lperror value\*(rp"
raises
.Ql "value"
as an error.
.Ql "try"
evaluates one body expression and returns its result if it succeeds.
If the body raises,
the handler’s
.Ql "\(lBe\(rB"
binds the raised value,
and
.Ql "try"
returns the handler’s result:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lptry 42
  \*(lp\(lBe\(rB 0\*(rp\*(rp                 ; 42

\*(lptry \*(lperror \(dqfailed\(dq\*(rp
  \*(lp\(lBe\(rB e\*(rp\*(rp                 ; \(dqfailed\(dq
.Ed
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr wattle 1
.Pp
The REPL’s
.Ql "\*(lpdoc\*(rp"
lists available bindings,
and
.Ql "\*(lpdoc symbol\*(rp"
describes one.
Example programs are distributed with the Wattle source tree.
.
.Sh HISTORY
Wattle began as a fork of Janet,
the language created by Calvin Rose.
.
